---
/**
 * The ordered checklist, and the page to read if you only read one.
 *
 * The order is set by how fast each step settles rather than by how large it is, so
 * every step closes with the same two-cell footer: the rung, and the horizon. Both
 * horizons quoted in the opening contrast are `T = (z·s/e)²` at 90% confidence — the
 * detection question belongs to `/how-sure/` and is not used here.
 */
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import MarginNote from "~/components/MarginNote.astro";
import Rail from "~/components/Rail.astro";
import Rung from "~/components/Rung.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Start here"
  description="The six things to do, in the order the size and the certainty of the payoff dictate. Fees first, because you would know in about four months. The clever parts last, because you would know in about thirty-one years."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <Rail>The order</Rail>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">Start here</h1>

    <MarginNote label="The question">
      <p>
        In what order should I do things, and how long before I would know whether each one worked? Checked
        against <code>docs/research/</code> on <span data-numeric>2026-08-23</span>.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Six steps. They are in this order because of how fast you would find out whether each one worked, which
    turns out to matter more than how big each one is.</p>

    <p>Step one settles in about four months. Step six settles in about thirty-one years. Both are worth roughly a
    percentage point a year on paper. Only one of them is a decision you can actually check.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Step 1</Rail>

    <h2>1. Pay less in fees</h2>

    <MarginNote label="Source">
      <p>
        Morningstar 2026, asset-weighted. The ICI series reads 0.05% against 0.64%. The equal-weighted active
        average of 1.00% to 1.12% overstates the gap by about 0.45 points, so the asset-weighted pair is the
        conservative one.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Open your employer plan and your brokerage account, and write down what every fund charges. Anything with a
    cheaper share class of the same thing, or a plain index equivalent, is a straight swap.</p>

    <p>Across the whole market, weighted by the money actually in them, index funds charge
    <Figure id="index-vs-active-fee" inline />. Booked conservatively against the fund an ordinary investor holds
    instead, moving is worth <Figure id="fee-gap" inline />.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Read from filings">
      <p>
        110 annual filings to the SEC covering 25 funds and eight fiscal years, as of
        <span data-numeric>2026-08-17</span>. SPY also has the tightest spread on the shelf, and a spread is paid
        once where a fee is paid every year.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>One wrinkle, because the sticker fee is not the cost. Funds lend their holdings out to short sellers and
    pass most of the fee back to shareholders, which offsets a fifth to a third of a broad index fund's charge. We
    read the real number out of 110 annual filings covering 25 funds. SPY, the most traded S&amp;P 500 fund in the
    world, is a trust that cannot lend at all, so it costs <Figure id="spy-net-cost" inline /> a year to own
    against 0.02% to 0.03% for every other fund tracking the same index. BND is the dearest broad bond fund on our
    shelf despite an identical 0.03% fee, for the same reason. Two funds with the same sticker are not the same
    purchase.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6 grid gap-5 border-t border-rule pt-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">How certain</p>
      <Rung level="settled" class="mt-1.5" />
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-[42ch] text-sm text-ink-muted">
        A fee is a contract. Nobody has to be right about markets for this to work.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">How long before you would know</p>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-[42ch] text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Immediately, and the whole fee-and-tax package reaches 90% confidence against your old portfolio in about
        four months.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Step 2</Rail>

    <h2>2. Fill the tax-advantaged space you already have</h2>

    <p>If your employer matches your contributions, take the whole match before you do anything else on this page.
    It is the only return here that arrives the same day you buy it.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Statute">
      <p>
        IRS Notice 2025-67, read <span data-numeric>2026-08-20</span>. The health savings account catch-up of
        $1,000 from age 55 has never been indexed. New Jersey is widely reported to tax the account the way
        California does, and we could not find a primary source saying so.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Then use the limits. For 2026 you can defer <Figure id="deferral-limit-2026" inline /> into a workplace
    plan, plus $8,000 more from age 50 and $11,250 in the years you are 60 to 63. An IRA takes $7,500, plus $1,100
    from 50. A health savings account takes <Figure id="hsa-limit-2026" inline />, plus $1,000 from 55, and it is
    the only account in the US code that is untaxed going in and untaxed coming out. California taxes it anyway,
    on all three legs.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Scope">
      <p>
        The menu cost is a range across the 15%, 18.8% and 23.8% rates, comparing a wholly captive plan with a
        fully self-directed one. It starts to bind once less than 55% of your sheltered money sits in accounts you
        control.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Two things that will catch a higher earner. The Roth IRA contribution disappears between $242,000 and
    $252,000 of joint income, so above that your Roth money has to come from a workplace Roth account or a
    conversion. And an old employer plan can be the most expensive thing you own, not because of its fees but
    because of its menu: no workplace plan offers the funds this research would put in a shelter, and when the
    menu binds it forces the fund that should be last in the queue into your best account. That costs
    <Figure id="captive-plan-cost" inline showUnit={false} /> a year, which is more than the entire account-placement decision is
    worth. Rolling an old plan into an IRA you control fixes it in one phone call.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6 grid gap-5 border-t border-rule pt-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">How certain</p>
      <Rung level="settled" class="mt-1.5" />
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-[42ch] text-sm text-ink-muted">These are statutes with numbers in them.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">How long before you would know</p>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-[42ch] text-sm text-ink-muted">Next April.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Step 3</Rail>

    <h2>3. Put the right funds in the right accounts</h2>

    <p>Same funds, same weights, different accounts, different result after tax. The rule everyone repeats is that
    bonds go in the IRA and international goes in the taxable account so you can claim the foreign tax credit.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Whose portfolio">
      <p>
        Derived for one investor holding equal nominal thirds in a Roth, a traditional account and a taxable
        brokerage account, at US federal rates only. State tax is excluded and shrinks every gap in the table
        without reordering it.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>For an investor holding roughly a third of their money in a Roth, a third in a traditional account and a
    third taxable, we measured the opposite. Every international fund we priced outranks every US equity fund in
    the queue for sheltered space, and a plain US total-market fund comes last at every tax rate tested.
    Sheltering the international holdings does permanently destroy the foreign tax credit, worth about 0.088% a
    year. It buys back more than that in ordinary income tax you stop paying.</p>

    <p>Then the honest part. Placement is worth <Figure id="placement-value" inline /> against a control an
    investor could actually build, and that is a long way below the 0.05% to 0.30% the industry quotes. Our own
    published headline used to be 0.38% to 0.55%. An internal re-derivation found lines measured against three
    different yardsticks added together, a cost avoided booked as a saving, and two mutually exclusive tactics
    both counted. The arithmetic had been right; what we booked from it was not.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6 grid gap-5 border-t border-rule pt-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">How certain</p>
      <Rung level="settled" class="mt-1.5" />
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-[42ch] text-sm text-ink-muted">
        The arithmetic is settled. Whether it applies to you moves with your brackets, your account sizes and what
        your employer plan will hold, so this is the one step you cannot copy from someone else.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">How long before you would know</p>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-[42ch] text-sm text-ink-muted">A year, on your 1099.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Step 4</Rail>

    <h2>4. Save more, and set your stock-versus-safe split from your life</h2>

    <MarginNote label="Scope">
      <p>
        The equivalence depends entirely on the ratio of your contributions to your balance, so it shrinks as the
        account grows: 2.57% over thirty years against 3.08% over thirty-six.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>The only research conclusion on this site that its own data can resolve is worth, over 36 years, about the
    same as raising your contributions by <Figure id="savings-equivalent" inline />. One budgeting decision,
    available this month, certain, with no fund risk in it at all.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Source">
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/FlanaganSe/kelly-portfolios/blob/main/docs/research/setting-the-equity-share.md"
          >Setting the equity share</a
        >: US, <span data-numeric>1963</span> to <span data-numeric>2025</span>, 750 months, with a modelled
        ten-year Treasury leg. One country, and every rung of the ladder takes 21 to 26 years.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Now the split. Pure growth arithmetic pushes toward holding all stocks, and it does that because it ignores
    everything that makes people sell. Choosing 60/40 instead is a forecast whether you write it down or not: it
    asserts that stocks will beat bonds by about 1.18% to 1.30% a year, against 5.51% a year realised from 1963 to
    2025. Moving from 60/40 to 90/10 measured <Figure id="equity-step" inline />, and 90% confidence in that took
    <Figure id="equity-step-horizon" inline />.</p>

    <p>So do not set the split from a return forecast. Set it from your withdrawals, your debts, whether your job
    disappears in the same recession that takes your portfolio, and the worst year you would genuinely sit through
    without selling. The single most valuable number missing from this entire project is your tolerable
    peak-to-trough fall. Nothing here can supply it.</p>

    <p>And do not assume the safe side is safe. Over 1963 to 2025 an all-bond portfolio fell 25.1% and stayed
    under water for 65 months, which is deeper and longer than the 30/70 mix's 17.9% over 41 months.</p>
  </div>

  <Callout kind="open-question">
    <p>
      This research models a portfolio rather than a household. If you carry debt costing more than the after-tax
      Treasury yield, paying it down is very likely the best return available to you for the risk you take, and no
      fund on any shelf competes with it. Nothing here prices that comparison.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <div class="mt-6 grid gap-5 border-t border-rule pt-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">How certain</p>
      <Rung level="settled" class="mt-1.5" />
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-[42ch] text-sm text-ink-muted">
        The savings arithmetic is settled. The split is a judgement about you, informed by measurement.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">How long before you would know</p>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-[42ch] text-sm text-ink-muted">
        24 years, which is the argument for not agonising over it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Step 5</Rail>

    <h2>5. Leave it alone, and rebalance with new money</h2>

    <p>Pick one month of the year. Check then, and only then. Act only when a holding has drifted at least a
    quarter away from its target, so a 20% target means you move at 15% or 25%, and do the trades inside your
    sheltered accounts where a sale costs no tax.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Source">
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/FlanaganSe/kelly-portfolios/blob/main/docs/research/rebalancing-policy.md"
          >Rebalancing policy</a
        >: 427 months, eight holdings, sheltered trades only, as of <span data-numeric>2026-08-21</span>. Roughly
        2.9 trades a year and no realisation tax.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>On the eight-holding portfolio over 427 months, that policy held average drift to
    <Figure id="rebalance-drift" inline />, at roughly three trades a year and no realisation tax at all. Left
    completely alone, the same portfolio drifted 6.39 points on average, and its trend holding wandered to 39.17%
    of the portfolio against a 30% target. Over 149 years, a drifting 60/40 ends up 86.3% invested in the United
    States. Buy-and-hold does not hold a global portfolio. It holds whichever market won.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Read as a shrug">
      <p>
        Against a smallest detectable difference of 1.81 percentage points, so this is a shrug with a number
        attached rather than a finding of zero. A separate 420-month measurement of a monthly policy came out at
        −0.39% a year.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Do not expect this to make you money. Measured against simply never trading, the policy returned
    <Figure id="rebalance-return" inline />, and the smallest difference that test could have seen is 1.81
    percentage points. We cannot tell whether rebalancing helps or hurts your return, and one earlier 35-year
    measurement came out slightly negative. Rebalance to control how much risk you are carrying, which is settled
    and is the actual reason to do it.</p>

    <p>Two traps worth precommitting against. Never move your review month once you have picked it, because the
    measured result moves 0.19 percentage points a year depending on whether you anchor in December, June or
    March, which is as large as the thing being measured, and because moving it is indistinguishable from reacting
    to a fall. And never set a performance trigger on a holding you bought as ballast: at a 30% weight such a
    holding trails in 43.8% of simulated ten-year histories even when the extra return it is supposed to earn is
    really there, so the trigger deletes it precisely for doing its job.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6 grid gap-5 border-t border-rule pt-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">How certain</p>
      <div class="mt-1.5 flex flex-col gap-1.5">
        <Rung level="settled" showGloss />
        <Rung level="cant-tell" showGloss />
      </div>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-[42ch] text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Settled on drift control, which is the reason to do it. We can't tell on return.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">How long before you would know</p>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-[42ch] text-sm text-ink-muted">
        The drift control works in the first year. The return question is not answerable on any data we have.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Step 6</Rail>

    <h2>6. Only now, the optional extras</h2>

    <p>Everything above is arithmetic. Everything here is a bet, and we have tested 21 separate ideas to arrive at
    the two worth your attention.</p>

    <MarginNote label="The gap in it">
      <p>
        86% of this result comes from data starting <span data-numeric>1990-11</span>, and nobody has ever run it
        on years the idea had not already seen. Charge each fund the shortfall we measured for it and the number
        falls to 0.60% or 0.30%, depending on the method.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Leaning your stock holdings toward cheap and profitable companies probably beats a cheap index fund by
    <Figure id="tilt-gap" inline />, and the honest range is 0.30 to 1.32. That clears the smallest difference the
    test could see, narrowly, once you use error bars that allow for markets going quiet for years and then going
    wild. It would take <Figure id="tilt-horizon" inline showUnit={false} /> before a study could separate that gap from zero, and
    86% of the result rests on data beginning in November 1990, which is also the only data the idea has ever been
    tested on.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Two questions">
      <p>
        Thirty years is the <em>detection</em> horizon, and thirty-one years is how long <em>your own account</em>
        would take. They are different questions that happen to land near each other here.
        <a href="/how-sure/">How sure we are</a> keeps them apart.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>The whole recommended portfolio, that lean plus a trend fund, reads 2.20 percentage points a year ahead of
    an index fund that borrows as much as it does. The range runs 0.05 to 4.57, the smallest difference the design
    could see is 2.83, and the honest verdict is that we cannot tell. Set it against a plain cheap index fund
    holding the same mix and your own account would need
    <Figure id="time-to-know-portfolio" inline showUnit={false} /> to settle it, which is the thirty-one years at
    the top of this page.</p>

    <p>If you do add anything, notice how you are paying for it. Selling 25% of your stocks to buy a trend fund
    and buying a fund that holds the stocks and the trend position at once, using borrowing inside the fund, are
    completely different purchases. The gap between them is <Figure id="funding-rule-swing" inline /> in the
    arithmetic, which is larger than any extra return this project has ever measured. Confusing the two has
    flipped more conclusions here than any other single mistake.</p>

    <p>And know what this feels like before you buy it. Owning cheap US stocks rather than the whole US market has
    run <Figure id="value-tilt-drought" inline /> and still has not caught up. That is not a simulated worst case.
    That is the record, and you would have been checking it every month.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6 grid gap-5 border-t border-rule pt-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">How certain</p>
      <div class="mt-1.5 flex flex-col gap-1.5">
        <Rung level="probably" showGloss />
        <Rung level="cant-tell" showGloss />
      </div>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-[42ch] text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Probably on the lean toward cheap and profitable companies. Can't tell on the whole portfolio, which is
        the honest verdict and not a no.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">How long before you would know</p>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-[42ch] text-sm text-ink-muted">
        About thirty-one years, against a cheap core holding the same split. Your own account is never going to be
        the evidence for this step.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <Callout kind="do-this">
    <p>
      Do the first three. Then decide about the rest with your eyes open, and read
      <a href="/portfolio/">the portfolio</a> before you buy anything on this list.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Base>
